<Base title="How the county works">
  <PageHead eyebrow="Learn" title="How the county works" lead="Since 2013, Nyamira has had its own government, with its own money and its own responsibilities." crumbs={[{ href: '/learn/', label: 'Learn' }, { href: '/learn/how-it-works/', label: 'How the county works' }]} />
  <div class="sw" lang="sw" style="margin-top:16px"><span class="eyebrow">Muhtasari kwa Kiswahili</span><p>Serikali ya kaunti ina sehemu mbili: Serikali Kuu ya Kaunti inayoongozwa na Gavana, inayotoa huduma na kutumia pesa; na Bunge la Kaunti, linalotunga sheria, kuidhinisha bajeti na kusimamia Serikali Kuu.</p></div>

  <ol class="stack" style="margin-top:16px; padding-left:20px">
    <li><strong>Two arms.</strong> The County Executive, led by the Governor, runs services and spends money. The County Assembly, made of MCAs, makes laws, approves the budget and checks the Executive.</li>
    <li><strong>Its own list of jobs.</strong> The Constitution gives the county 14 functions, including county health services, pre-primary education, county roads, agriculture, markets and water services. <a href="/constitution/fourth-schedule/">See the list</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Its own money.</strong> Most of it comes from the national government as a share of national revenue. The county also collects its own revenue from fees, permits and charges.</li>
    <li><strong>Its own places.</strong> Nyamira has 4 sub-counties and 20 wards. Each ward elects one MCA.</li>
    <li><strong>The national government is still here.</strong> Police, chiefs, primary and secondary schools, and national roads belong to the national government. <a href="/government/whos-in-charge/">Who's in charge?</a></li>
    <li><strong>You have a say.</strong> The law requires the county to involve residents in budgets, plans and laws. <a href="/learn/taking-part/">How to take part</a>.</li>
  </ol>
</Base>
